Kreative Studio pose editor (STU-PE1). Plain JavaScript, no libraries, no build step. The same file runs in Node 22 (CommonJS require) and in the browser (classic <script>, no modules). Use exactly this wrapper and nothing outside it:

(function () {
  'use strict';
  const NS = typeof module === 'object' && module.exports ? null : (globalThis.KKPose = globalThis.KKPose || {});
  const need = (name, file) => (NS ? NS[name] : require(file));
  // ... the code; get other pose modules with need('vec', './vec.js') INSIDE functions (load order in the browser is free) ...
  const api = { /* exported names */ };
  if (NS) NS.<NAME> = api; else module.exports = api;
})();

Angles are degrees in every API. Coordinates are metres: +X is the figure's left, -Y its front, +Z up. Matrices are arrays of 9 numbers, row-major. Do not change the test file.


Write public/js/pose/depth.js (NAME = depth): the depth picture of the posed mannequin for ControlNet depth, near = white, far = dark grey, background black. Use need('vec','./vec.js') (V), need('project','./project.js') (P) and need('depthParts','./depth-parts.js') inside the functions. w = fk.solve(pose) = {pos, rot}.

Export render(view, w, img) and primitives (= the depthParts primitives function, re-exported: primitives: (w) => need('depthParts','./depth-parts.js').primitives(w)). Each shape of primitives(w) is {type:'capsule', a, b, r} or {type:'ellipsoid', c, rot, radii} in world metres.
render(view, w, img): ray-cast every pixel with an orthographic ray along the view depth. Move each primitive into screen space with P.toScreen (points) and radii times view.s; an ellipsoid's axes become Ms * rot (Ms below). For pixel (px, py) the ray is (x, y) = (px + 0.5, py + 0.5), depth t free; keep the largest t (nearest) over all primitives, scanning only each primitive's screen bounding box (centre +- its largest radius, or both capsule ends +- r).
- sphere (centre C, radius r): hit when dx*dx + dy*dy <= r*r with dx = x - C[0], dy = y - C[1]: t = C[2] + sqrt(r*r - dx*dx - dy*dy).
- capsule (A, B, r): the two end spheres, plus the cylinder: d = norm(B - A), L = len(B - A), w0 = [x - A[0], y - A[1], -A[2]], k = [0,0,1]; q0 = w0 - d*dot(w0,d), q1 = k - d*dot(k,d); a = dot(q1,q1), b = 2*dot(q0,q1), c = dot(q0,q0) - r*r; when a > 1e-12 and b*b - 4*a*c >= 0: t = (-b + sqrt(b*b - 4ac)) / (2a); it counts when 0 <= dot(w0 + t*k, d) <= L.
- ellipsoid (centre C, axes matrix M = Ms * rot, radii scaled): o = M^T * [x - C[0], y - C[1], -C[2]] divided per axis by the radius, k = M^T * [0,0,1] divided per axis by the radius; solve |o + t k|^2 = 1, keep the larger root t.
Ms: P.toScreen maps view space (x right, z up, y away) to screen (x right, y down, depth toward the camera), so with R0, R1, R2 the rows of view.R, Ms is the 3x3 matrix with rows R0, -R2, -R1 (as 9 numbers: [...R0, ...-R2, ...-R1]).
After all pixels: zmin/zmax over the hit pixels; each hit pixel gets g = Math.round(255 * (0.15 + 0.85 * (t - zmin) / (zmax - zmin))) (255 when zmax === zmin) in R, G and B; misses stay as they are (img comes in black).
